### The resulting architecture

### Peridial Minting — three-layer integrity model

HI — Authority and Recognition

Account integrity · Credential authorization · Issuer governance · Evidence qualification

ME — Generation and Synchronization

Card Conduit Generator · Peridial Minting · Cryptographic binding · Clock profile · Credential recognition

LO — Blockchain and Preservation

Blockchain anchors · PPSA · Document provenance · Revocation history · Recovery checkpoints

All three layers operate under CBBC authorization and bounded autonomy.

### 8\. Initial acceptance criteria

The first implementation should demonstrate that:

* An unauthorized issuer cannot mint a valid credential.

* A credential cannot be bound to an account without authorization.

* Any modification to a signed credential is detected.

* A generated Card Conduit resolves to its correct provenance record.

* Blockchain anchors match the corresponding off-chain records.

* Revoked credentials fail subsequent validity checks.

* Recovery requires a separate authorization certificate.

* A credential's historical audit trail survives suspension and revocation.

* Clock synchronization failure does not silently invalidate or rewrite historical issuance timestamps.

* A verifier can confirm required claims without receiving the entire private account record.

Recommended first deliverable: Peridial Minting Technical Specification v0.1, covering the Card Conduit Generator, credential manifest, account-binding protocol, blockchain event schema, and CBBC validator interactions.

The defining doctrine:

> Recognize through credentials. Bind through cryptography. Generate through verified conduits. Anchor through consensus. Preserve through provenance.

A useful next step would be to establish a testable Card Conduit Generator specification and its credential-card field layout.
